<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Danish translation.

    A translation of `values/strings.xml`, which is the source (SPEC §9). It
    serves the five Danish networks of the catalogue — Copenhagen, Aarhus,
    Odense, Aalborg and Ballerup — and, like every other language here, any
    other city the reader installs. Where Android's own Danish has a word, that
    word is used; the arbiter is the phone itself.

    Rules, the same as everywhere: positional placeholders, <plurals> for
    anything that agrees, and the `name` attributes left untouched. Danish
    distinguishes two plural categories, `one` and `other`.

    The typography is Danish: quotation marks are » «, closing inwards, and
    the dash that breaks a sentence is – with a space on either side rather
    than the em dash the English file uses. Nothing needs escaping: no straight
    apostrophe is written anywhere in the file. Compounds are written as one
    word, which is what lengthens the labels; where a button could not carry
    the literal translation it takes the shorter wording Android itself uses.
    The reader is addressed as `du`, the only address modern Danish has, and
    the impersonal turns of the English are kept impersonal.
    See docs/glossary/da.md.
-->
